ion\")\n\nThese are independent, so I can batch them.\n\nThen write the test file.\n\nTest file structure:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nfrom typesystem.base import ParseError, Position\nfrom typesystem.tokenize import tokenize_yaml\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokens import DictToken, ListToken, ScalarToken\n\nimport typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml as tokenize_yaml_module\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_requires_pyyaml(monkeypatch):\n    monkeypatch.setattr(tokenize_yaml_module, \"yaml\", None)\n    with pytest.raises(AssertionError) as exc:\n        tokenize_yaml_yaml(\"123\")\n    assert \"'pyyaml' must be installed.\" in str(exc.value)\n```\n\nWait \u2014 careful: `from typesystem.tokenize import tokenize_yaml` \u2014 is tokenize_yaml exported from typesystem.tokenize? In typesystem, `typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml` module has `tokenize_yaml` function. There's also `typesystem.tokenize.__init__` which might import from tokenize_json. Let me just import the module directly:\n\n```python\nimport typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml as yaml_tokenize\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml\n```\n\nHmm, but wait: does `typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml` exist as importable? The file is typesystem/tokenize/tokenize_yaml.py, and typesystem/tokenize/__init__.py exists (it has tokenize_json, tokenize_yaml functions maybe re-exported). Yes, in typesystem 0.4.x, there's typesystem/tokenize/__init__.py:\n\n```python\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_json import tokenize_json\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml\n```\n\nHmm, not sure. But importing the module directly by path works regardless.\n\nNow the tests:\n\n```python\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_bytes():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(b'{\"key\": \"value\"}')\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.value == {\"key\": \"value\"}\n```\n\nWait \u2014 token.value for DictToken: get_value returns {key.get_value(): value.get_value()}. Keys are ScalarToken(\"key\"), values 