th most indicators...\n        if ch in '#,[]{}&*!|>\\'%@`':\n            return False\n```\n\nYes, '@' and '`' are in the excluded list, so they fall through to the error. Good.\n\nAlternatively, to be safer, I could use a different malformed YAML that definitely raises ScannerError or ParserError, and just assert code == \"parse_error\" without asserting exact text (to avoid brittleness across PyYAML versions). But asserting the text is a good postcondition check. I could assert `error.text.endswith(\".\")` and that position is set. Hmm. Let me assert code and that position is a Position with char_index 0, and text contains \"cannot start any token\". Actually, let me be a bit flexible: assert error.code == \"parse_error\" and error.position == Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0). And maybe assert \"cannot start any token\" in error.text. That's robust.\n\nAlso test a ParserError case: e.g. `\"{invalid\"` \u2192 ParserError \"while parsing a flow mapping ... expected ',' or '}'\". The problem for ParserError: exc.problem = \"expected ',' or '}'\" hmm, actually for ParserError the problem might be like \"expected ',' or '}', but got ...\" Let me not assert exact text for that one; just code == \"parse_error\". Actually, including both ScannerError and ParserError tests improves branch coverage of the except clause (both exception types). The except tuple covers both regardless; but testing both is nice.\n\nLet me think about what `\"{invalid\"` produces: yaml.load(\"{invalid\", SafeLoader) \u2192 ParserError: \"while parsing a flow mapping\\nexpected ',' or '}', but got '<stream end>'\"? The problem attribute would be \"expected ',' or '}', but got ''\"? Hmm, ParserError problem: in parser.py, `expect` raises ParserError(\"while parsing a flow mapping\", start_mark, \"expected ',' or '}'\", self.get_mark())? Something like that. The exact text varies. I'll just assert code == \"parse_error\" and that a position is present.\n\nActually wait \u2014 for ParserError, `exc.problem_mark` exists. Yes, ParserError extends MarkedYAMLError which has problem_mark. Good.\n\nHmm, one more consideration: for `\"{invalid\"`, problem_mark.index \u2014 the mark at end of stream, index 8. Position would be line 1, column 9, char_index 8. I won't assert exact values, just that it's a Position instance. Or compute expected: Position(line_no=1, column_no=9, char_index=8). Hmm, risky. Let me just assert isinstance(error.position, Position).\n\nActually, let me reconsider: maybe assert exact for the '@' case (well-known) and just code for the ParserError case.\n\nNow, mapping/sequence/scalar/int/float/bool/null tests. Let me write them to also verify positions and to_string, ensuring the constructors' logic (start, end-1) is correct.\n\nFor `tokenize_yaml('{\"a\": 1}')`:\n- content = '{\"a\": 1}', len 8.\n- Top-level flow mapping node: start_mark.index = 0, end_mark.index = 8. DictToken(mapping, 0, 7).\n- token.to_string() = content[0:8] = '{\"a\": 1}'. \n- token.value == {\"a\": 1}.\n- token.start == Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0).\n- token.end == Position(line_no=1, column_no=8, char_index=7).\n\nWait, end index 7 \u2192 column_no = 7 - rfind(\"\\n\", 0, 7) = 7 - (-1) = 8. Yes.\n\nHmm, but is end_mark.index for a flow mapping the index after '}'? For '{\"a\": 1}', the '}' is at index 7, so end_mark.index = 8 (pointing after '}'). So end - 1 = 7 \u2192 to_string includes '}'. Good.\n\nFor nested: `tokenize_yaml('{\"a\": {\"b\": 2}}')` \u2014 nested DictToken. token.value == {\"a\": {\"b\": 2}}. Also can check inner token: token._value? Hmm, accessing private. Better: use token.value for the dict, and maybe check that the inner value is a dict. To verify nested tokens are DictToken instances, I could access `token._value` \u2014 private but fine in tests? Maybe better to avoid. Alternatively use `token.get_value()`... same thing.\n\nHmm, actually there might be a public way: DictToken stores _value as dict of tokens. There's no public accessor for the raw tokens I think. Let me check get_info for DictToken to see its methods.\n\nActually, in typesystem's tokens.py, I recall DictToken has:\n\n```python\nclass DictToken(Token):\n    def _get_value(self):\n        return {\n            key.get_value(): value.get_value()\n            for key, value in self._value.items()\n        }\n\n    def get_value(self):\n        return self._get_value()\n```\n\nHmm, or maybe it's directly in get_value. Let me check with get_info(\"DictToken\") and get_info(\"DictToken.get_value\").\n\nAlso ScalarToken might have `.string` property? Let me check.\n\nLet me also check `Token.to_string`, `Token.start`, `Token.end`.\n\nOK let me make the get_info calls now. I'll batch:\n\n1. get_info(\"tokenize_yaml\")\n2. get_info(\"DictToken\")\n3. get_info(\"ListToken\")\n4. get_info(\"ScalarToken\")\n5. get_info(\"Token\")\n6. get_info(\"ParseError\")\n7. get_info(\"Position\")\n8. get_info(\"_get_position\")\n\nThat's 8 calls in one block. Then based on results, write tests.\n\nActually, I realize the get_info tool might be scoped to the module under test (typesystem/tokenize/tokenize_yaml.py), so \"DictToken\" might resolve since it's imported there. Let's try.\n\nAfter getting info, write the test script.\n\nDraft test script:\n\n```python\nimport pytest\n\nimport typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml as tokenize_yaml_module\nfrom typesystem.base import ParseError, Position\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml\nfrom typesystem.tokenize.tokens import DictToken, ListToken, ScalarToken\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_requires_pyyaml(monkeypatch):\n    monkeypatch.setattr(tokenize_yaml_module, \"yaml\", None)\n    with pytest.raises(AssertionError) as exc_info:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"123\")\n    assert \"'pyyaml' must be installed.\" in str(exc_info.value)\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_empty_string():\n    with pytest.raises(ParseError) as exc_info:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"\")\n    error = exc_info.value\n    assert error.text == \"No content.\"\n    assert error.code == \"no_content\"\n    assert error.position == Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0)\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_whitespace_only():\n    with pytest.raises(ParseError) as exc_info:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"  \\n\\t \")\n    error = exc_info.value\n    assert error.code == \"no_content\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_bytes_content():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(b'{\"key\": \"value\"}')\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.value == {\"key\": \"value\"}\n    assert token.to_string() == '{\"key\": \"value\"}'\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_bytes_multibyte():\n    token = tokenize_yaml('\"caf\\xc3\\xa9\"'.encode(\"utf-8\"))\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == \"caf\u00e9\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_mapping():\n    token = tokenize_yaml('{\"a\": 1}')\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.value == {\"a\": 1}\n    assert token.to_string() == '{\"a\": 1}'\n    assert token.start == Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0)\n    assert token.end == Position(line_no=1, column_no=8, char_index=7)\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_nested_mapping():\n    token = tokenize_yaml('{\"a\": {\"b\": [1, true, null]}}')\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.value == {\"a\": {\"b\": [1, True, None]}}\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_block_mapping():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"key: value\\nother: 2\")\n    assert isinstance(token, DictToken)\n    assert token.value == {\"key\": \"value\", \"other\": 2}\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_sequence():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"[1, 2, 3]\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ListToken)\n    assert token.value == [1, 2, 3]\n    assert token.to_string() == \"[1, 2, 3]\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_block_sequence():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"- a\\n- b\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ListToken)\n    assert token.value == [\"a\", \"b\"]\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_string_scalar():\n    token = tokenize_yaml('\"hello\"')\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == \"hello\"\n    assert token.to_string() == '\"hello\"'\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_plain_scalar():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"hello\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == \"hello\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_int():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"123\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == 123\n    assert token.to_string() == \"123\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_float():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"1.5\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value == 1.5\n    assert token.to_string() == \"1.5\"\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_bool():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"true\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value is True\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_null():\n    token = tokenize_yaml(\"null\")\n    assert isinstance(token, ScalarToken)\n    assert token.value is None\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_scanner_error():\n    with pytest.raises(ParseError) as exc_info:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"@invalid\")\n    error = exc_info.value\n    assert error.code == \"parse_error\"\n    assert \"cannot start any token\" in error.text\n    assert error.position == Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0)\n\n\ndef test_tokenize_yaml_parser_error():\n    with pytest.raises(ParseError) as exc_info:\n        tokenize_yaml(\"{invalid\")\n    error = exc_info.value\n    assert error.code == \"parse_error\"\n    assert isinstance(error.position, Position)\n```\n\nWait \u2014 careful with `test_tokenize_yaml_bytes_multibyte`: `'\"caf\\xc3\\xa9\"'.encode(\"utf-8\")` \u2014 the string '\"caf\\xc3\\xa9\"' in Python source is the 6-char string `\"caf\u00c9\"`? \\xc3\\xa9 in a str literal are codepoints U+00C3 U+00E9, encoding to utf-8 gives b'\"caf\\xc3\\x83\\xc2\\xa9\"'. That's wrong. I should write: `tokenize_yaml('\"caf\u00e9\"'.encode(\"utf-8\"))` \u2192 b'\"caf\\xc3\\xa9\"'