e_yaml first and _get_position after? Let me think about the line numbers: the snippet shows line 26 as the assert, line 28 `if isinstance(content, bytes):`. The def tokenize_yaml line would be ~24-25. Before that: imports (typing, typesystem.base, typesystem.tokenize.tokens, try/except yaml import). That's about:\n\n```\n1: import typing\n2: (blank)\n3: from typesystem.base import ParseError, Position\n4: from typesystem.tokenize.tokens import DictToken, ListToken, ScalarToken, Token\n5: (blank)\n6: try:\n7:     import yaml\n8:     from yaml.loader import SafeLoader\n9: except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover\n10:     yaml = None\n11: (blank)\n12: (blank)\n13: def _get_position(content, index):\n14:     return Position(\n15:         line_no=...\n16:         column_no=...\n17:         char_index=index,\n18:     )\n19: (blank)\n20: (blank)\n21: def tokenize_yaml(content):\n22:     assert yaml is not None...\n```\n\nHmm, that gives assert at line 22, not 26. With more blank lines or docstring, could be 26. Whatever \u2014 the exact layout doesn't matter much. The key question: is _get_position before or after tokenize_yaml, and is its body covered?\n\nGiven the task says \"lines 26-109 do not execute\", and the snippet shows lines 26-109 as the entire tokenize_yaml body, I'll assume _get_position is either covered elsewhere or not required. My tests will cover _get_position anyway (via the parse error path), which can't hurt.\n\nHmm wait, actually there's another possibility: the except clause calls `_get_position` \u2014 if _get_position is defined AFTER tokenize_yaml in the file (lines 111+), then at the time tokenize_yaml runs, _get_position is already defined at module level (since the whole module is imported first). That works fine in Python. And the coverage statement \"lines 26-109 do not execute\" would then be consistent with _get_position's body being at 111+ and also uncovered but not mentioned... The task says \"Create new pytest test functions that execute all missing lines and branches\" \u2014 focusing on 26-109. My tests will cover the parse error path which calls _get_position, so if it's at 111+, it gets covered too. \n\nNow, let me also think about whether `yaml.loader.SafeLoader` import works \u2014 yes, standard.\n\nOne more consideration: the `construct_scalar` for keys. In `loader.construct_mapping(node)`, PyYAML's SafeConstructor.construct_mapping calls construct_object for key and value nodes. For a key scalar \"a\" (str tag), our construct_scalar is called \u2192 returns ScalarToken. Then SafeConstructor.construct_mapping uses the key... wait, does it hash the key? construct_mapping does:\n\n```python\ndef construct_mapping(self, node, deep=False):\n    if isinstance(node, MappingNode):\n        self.flatten_mapping(node)\n    return super().construct_mapping(node, deep=deep)\n```\n\nBaseConstructor.construct_mapping:\n\n```python\ndef construct_mapping(self, node, deep=False):\n    if isinstance(node, MappingNode):\n        ...\n    mapping = {}\n    for key_node, value_node in node.value:\n        key = self.construct_object(key_node, deep=deep)\n        if not isinstance(key, collections.abc.Hashable):\n            raise ...\n        value = self.construct_object(value_node, deep=deep)\n        mapping[key] = value\n    return mapping\n```\n\nSo key = ScalarToken instance \u2014 is it hashable? Token doesn't define __hash__... but it defines __eq__! If a class defines __eq__ without __hash__, Python sets __hash__ to None \u2192 unhashable! \n\nHmm wait, that would break construct_mapping. Unless Token defines __hash__ or __eq__ is not defined... Let me think. In typesystem tokens.py, does Token define __eq__? If it does and no __hash__, then ScalarToken would be unhashable and construct_mapping would raise \"unhashable type\" TypeError.\n\nHmm, but typesystem's tokenize_yaml works in practice (it's a real library with tests). So either Token defines __hash__, or __eq__ is not defined on Token.\n\nLet me recall typesystem/tokenize/tokens.py more carefully. From the actual repo (encode/typesystem, master):\n\n```python\nimport typing\n\nfrom typesystem.base import Position\n\n\nclass Token:\n    def __init__(self, value, start_index, end_index, content=None):\n        assert content is not None or isinstance(value, (str, bytes)), (\n            \"Must pass either content=..., or value must be a string.\"\n        )\n        self._value = value\n        self._start_index = start_index\n        self._end_index = end_index\n        self._content = content\n\n    def __repr__(self) -> str:\n        return \"%s(%r)\" % (self.__class__.__name__, self.value)\n\n    def __eq__(self, other: typing.Any) -> bool:\n        return self.value == other.value\n\n    def __hash__(self) -> int:\n        return hash(self.value)\n\n    @property\n    def value(self) -> typing.Any:\n        return self.get_value()\n\n    @property\n    def start(self) -> Position:\n        return self._get_position(self._start_index)\n\n    @property\n    def end(self) -> Position:\n        return self._get_position(self._end_index)\n\n    def _get_position(self, index: int) -> Position:\n        return Position(\n            line_no=self._content.count(\"\\n\", 0, index) + 1,\n            column_no=index - self._content.rfind(\"\\n\", 0, index),\n            char_index=index,\n        )\n\n    def get_value(self) -> typing.Any:\n        return self._value\n\n    def is_scalar(self) -> bool:\n        return isinstance(self, ScalarToken)\n\n    def is_dict(self) -> bool:\n        return isinstance(self, DictToken)\n\n    def is_list(self) -> bool:\n        return isinstance(self, ListToken)\n\n    def to_string(self) -> str:\n        return self._content[self._start_index : self._end_index + 1]\n\n\nclass ScalarToken(Token):\n    def has_value(self, value: str) -> bool:\n        return str(self.value) == value\n\n\nclass DictToken(Token):\n    def get_value(self) -> typing.Any:\n        return {\n            key.get_value(): value.get_value() for key, value in self._value.items()\n        }\n\n\nclass ListToken(Token):\n    def get_value(self) -> typing.Any:\n        return [value.get_value() for value in self._value]\n```\n\nI'm fairly confident Token defines both __eq__ and __hash__ (hash of value). OK good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a subtlety with construct_mapping and duplicate keys: if two keys have equal values (e.g. {\"a\": 1, \"a\": 2}), the ScalarTokens hash equal \u2192 dict dedupes. Fine.\n\nAnother subtlety: SafeConstructor.construct_mapping calls self.flatten_mapping(node) for merge keys (<<). flatten_mapping calls construct_object on keys... For simple YAML, no merges. Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 important: does `loader.construct_mapping(node)` in our custom construct_mapping recurse properly? Yes: BaseConstructor.construct_mapping \u2192 construct_object(key_node) \u2192 looks up constructor by tag \u2192 our custom constructors \u2192 returns Tokens. \n\nBut hmm, one thing: `construct_object` has a `deep` parameter and caching (self.constructed_objects). For non-deep construction of nested collections, PyYAML constructs generators... Actually for mappings/sequences, construct_object returns the generator-based partial? Let me think: BaseConstructor.construct_object:\n\n```python\ndef construct_object(self, node, deep=False):\n    if node in self.constructed_objects:\n        return self.constructed_objects[node]\n    if deep:\n        old_deep = self.deep_construct\n        self.deep_construct = True\n    if node in self.recursive_objects:\n        raise ConstructorError(...)\n    self.recursive_objects[node] = None\n    constructor = None\n    tag_suffix = None\n    if node.tag in self.yaml_constructors:\n        constructor = self.yaml_constructors[node.tag]\n    else:\n        ...\n    if tag_suffix is None:\n        data = constructor(self, node)\n    else:\n        data = constructor(self, tag_suffix, node)\n    if isinstance(data, types.GeneratorType):\n        generator = data\n        data = next(generator)\n        if self.deep_construct:\n            for dummy in generator:\n                pass\n        else:\n            self.state_generators.append(generator)\n    self.constructed_objects[node] = data\n    del self.recursive_objects[node]\n    if deep:\n        self.deep_construct = old_deep\n    return data\n```\n\nOur constructors return Token objects directly (not generators), so data = Token. Good. And nested nodes get constructed via the same registry since we call loader.construct_mapping/construct_sequence which call construct_object on children. \n\nWait, but there's a catch: BaseConstructor.construct_mapping is called with deep=False by default from our construct_mapping (`loader.construct_mapping(node)`). Then for value nodes that are collections, construct_object(value_node, deep=False) \u2192 our construct_mapping returns DictToken immediately (not a generator), so no deferred construction. All good \u2014 nested tokens are fully constructed.\n\nHmm, wait: actually SafeConstructor.construct_mapping signature: `def construct_mapping(self, node, deep=False)`. Our call `loader.construct_mapping(node)` passes deep=False. Fine.\n\nNow, `flatten_mapping` \u2014 SafeConstructor.construct_mapping:\n\n```python\ndef construct_mapping(self, node, deep=False):\n    if isinstance(node, MappingNode):\n        self.flatten_mapping(node)\n    return BaseConstructor.construct_mapping(self, node, deep=deep)\n```\n\nflatten_mapping handles merge keys '<<'. For keys with tag merge... it calls construct_object(key_node) for merge keys. Not relevant for simple tests.\n\nOK now \u2014 one more potential gotcha: `construct_scalar` is registered for DEFAULT_SCALAR_TAG which is \"tag:yaml.org,2002:str\". But PyYAML's resolver resolves plain scalars to specific tags (int, float, bool, null, timestamp, etc.) via implicit resolution. Quoted scalars get the str tag. Plain \"hello\" \u2192 str tag. Plain \"123\" \u2192 int tag. So:\n\n- construct_scalar: called for quoted strings and plain strings that don't match other resolvers. E.g. `hello`, `\"hello\"`, `'world'`.\n- construct_int: `123`, `0x1F`, etc.\n- construct_float: `1.5`, `.inf`, etc.\n- construct_bool: `true`, `yes`, `on`... (PyYAML SafeLoader bool resolver: yes/no/true/false/on/off).\n- construct_null: `null`, `~`, empty.\n\nAlso note: timestamps! Plain scalars like `2020-01-01` resolve to tag:yaml.org,2002:timestamp, and SafeConstructor has a timestamp constructor \u2014 but we didn't override it, so it returns a datetime.date, not a ScalarToken. Not needed for coverage.\n\nAlso binary tag, etc. Not needed.\n\nNow, what about the top-level document being a scalar with the str tag: `tokenize_yaml(\"hello\")` \u2192 root node is ScalarNode with tag str \u2192 construct_scalar \u2192 ScalarToken(\"hello\", 0, 4). value \"hello\". Good.\n\nEdge: `tokenize_yaml(\"123\")` \u2192 construct_int \u2192 ScalarToken(123, 0, 2). to_string \"123\". Good.\n\nNow let me also think about `test_tokenize_yaml_bool` with \"true\": construct_yaml_bool returns True. ScalarToken(True, 0, 3). `token.value is True`. Good.\n\nAnd null: \"null\" \u2192 construct_yaml_null returns None. ScalarToken(None, 0, 3). value is None. Good.\n\nFloat: \"1.5\" \u2192 1.5. Also could test \".inf\" \u2192 float('inf'). Keep simple.\n\nNow, about asserting positions for the mapping test: token.start.char_index == 0, token.end.char_index == 7 for '{\"a\": 1}'. Let me double check end_mark for flow mapping: For input '{\"a\": 1}' (8 chars, indices 0-7), the mapping node's end_mark.index \u2014 the scanner produces FLOW-MAPPING-START at 0, ..., FLOW-MAPPING-END at index 7 (the '}' char). The end_mark of the MappingNode is the mark AFTER the '}' token, i.e., index 8. So end - 1 = 7 \u2192 to_string = content[0:8] = '{\"a\": 1}'. Good.\n\nFor block mapping \"key: value\\nother: 2\": the block mapping node start_mark.index = 0, end_mark.index = 18 (end of stream after \"other: 2\" which is 17 chars + newline = 18). end - 1 = 17 \u2192 to_string = content[0:18] = \"key: value\\nother: 2\". Good.\n\nFor block sequence \"- a\\n- b\": start 0, end 7 (5 chars + \\n = 6? \"- a\\n- b\" is 7 chars: '-',' ','a','\\n','-',' ','b' \u2192 indices 0-6, end of stream at 7). end-1 = 6 \u2192 to_string = content[0:7] = \"- a\\n- b\". Good.\n\nFor scalar \"123\": start_mark.index=0, end_mark.index=3 (after the scalar). end-1=2 \u2192 to_string = content[0:3] = \"123\". Good.\n\nFor '\"hello\"': scalar node from index 0 to 7 (after closing quote). end-1=6 \u2192 to_string = content[0:7] = '\"hello\"'. Good.\n\nNow positions: token.start for \"123\" \u2192 Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0). token.end \u2192 char_index=2, column_no=3.\n\nI'll assert a few of these but keep it robust. Actually, asserting exact positions is good for verifying postconditions. Let me assert char_index primarily, and maybe line/column for one multi-line case.\n\nMulti-line test: \n\n```python\ncontent = \"key: value\\nother: 2\"\ntoken = tokenize_yaml(content)\nassert token.start == Position(line_no=1, column_no=1, char_index=0)\nassert token.end == Position(line_no=2, column_no=9, char_index=17)\n```\n\nend char_index 17 \u2192 line_no = content.count(\"\\n\", 0, 17) + 1 = 1 + 1 = 2. column_no = 17 - content.rfind(\"\\n\", 0, 17) = 17 - 10 = 9. Yes: \"key: value\\n\" is 11 chars (indices 0-10), so rfind(\"\\n\", 0, 17) = 10. 17-10 = 9. \"other: 2\" \u2014 'o' at index 11 (column 2? hmm). Wait column_no=9 means the position is at the 9th column of line 2. Index 11 is 'o' \u2192 column 1... hmm, index 17 is the char after '2' (index 16 is '2'). So column of index 17 = 17 - 10 = 9 \u2192 9th column. Line 2 is \"other: 2\" (indices 11-18? no\u2014content length is 19: \"key: value\\nother: 2\" = 10 + 1 + 8 = 19 chars, indices 0-18). Wait: \"key: value\" is 10 chars (k,e,y,:,space,v,a,l,u,e \u2192 indices 0-9), \"\\n\" at index 10, \"other: 2\" is 8 chars (indices 11-18). End of stream mark index = 19. So end - 1 = 18 \u2192 to_string = content[0:19] = whole content. char_index=18 \u2192 line_no = count(\"\\n\",0,18)+1 = 2, column_no = 18 - rfind(\"\\n\",0,18) = 18 - 10 = 8. So token.end == Position(line_no=2, column_no=8, char_index=18).\n\nLet me redo: content = \"key: value\\nother: 2\", len = 19. Block mapping end_mark.index = 19 (end of stream). end - 1 = 18. to_string = content[0:19] = full content. \u2713.\n\ntoken.end = Position(line_no=2, column_no=8, char_index=18). \u2713 (index 18 is the last char '2', column 8 of line 2: \"other: 2\" \u2014 o=1,t=2,h=3,e=4,r=5,:=6,space=7,2=8. Yes column 8.)\n\nOK. I'll include this multi-line position assertion.\n\nNow, the ParseError assertions. Let me figure out how to access text/code/position. I'll check get_info for ParseError/BaseError/Message. If BaseError only has _messages, I'll use `error.messages[0]`.\n\nHmm, actually wait. Let me reconsider: maybe typesystem's BaseError does set self.text etc. Let me look at actual typesystem source (v0.4.1) base.py:\n\n```python\nclass BaseError(Exception):\n    def __init__(\n        self,\n        *,\n        text: str = None,\n        code: str = None,\n        position: Position = None,\n        messages: typing.List[Message] = None,\n    ) -> None:\n        if messages is None:\n            messages = [Message(text=text, code=code, position=position)]\n        self._messages = messages\n\n    @property\n    def messages(self) -> typing.List[Message]:\n        return self._messages\n    ...\n```\n\nYes, I'm quite sure there's no .text attribute on BaseError. So use `.messages[0].text` etc. But get_info will confirm.\n\nHmm, wait \u2014 actually I want to double-check because the task snippet raises ParseError(text=..., code=..., position=...). And typesystem's own tests for tokenize_yaml... let me recall tests/test_yaml.py in typesystem repo:\n\n```python\ndef test_parse_yaml_errors():\n    with pytest.raises(ParseErr