 def get_value(self):\n        if isinstance(self._value, dict):\n            return {key.get_value(): value.get_value() for key, value in self._value.items()}\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, I recall:\n\n```python\nclass Token:\n    def __init__(self, value, start_index, end_index, content=None):\n        self._value = value\n        self._start_index = start_index\n        self._end_index = end_index\n        self._content = content\n\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        return self.value == other.value\n\n    @property\n    def start(self) -> Position:\n        return self._get_position(self._start_index)\n\n    @property\n    def end(self) -> Position:\n        return self._get_position(self._end_index)\n\n    def _get_position(self, index):\n        return Position(...)\n\n    def get_value(self):\n        return self._value\n\n    def is_scalar(self):\n        return isinstance(self, ScalarToken)\n\n    def is_dict(self):\n        return isinstance(self, DictToken)\n\n    def is_list(self):\n        return isinstance(self, ListToken)\n\n    def to_string(self):\n        return self._content[self._start_index : self._end_index + 1]\n```\n\nAnd DictToken:\n\n```python\nclass DictToken(Token):\n    def get_value(self):\n        return {key.get_value(): value.get_value() for key, value in self._value.items()}\n```\n\nListToken:\n\n```python\nclass ListToken(Token):\n    def get_value(self):\n        return [value.get_value() for value in self._value]\n```\n\nScalarToken:\n\n```python\nclass ScalarToken(Token):\n    def has_value(self, value):\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm, I'm not 100% sure. Let me use get_info to check.\n\nAlso `_get_position` in tokenize_yaml.py:\n\n```python\ndef _get_position(content: str, index: int) -> Position:\n    return Position(\n        line_no=content.count(\"\\n\", 0, index) + 1,\n        column_no=index - content.rfind(\"\\n\", 0, index),\n        char_index=index,\n    )\n```\n\nThat's lines 1-24 presumably (before line 26). The uncovered lines are 26-109, so _get_position is presumably covered elsewhere or it's lines before 26. Actually the snippet starts at line 26 with the assert, so _get_position is defined before. The uncovered region is 26-109 which includes everything in tokenize_yaml.\n\nNow, for the tests:\n\n1. `test_tokenize_yaml_empty_string`: \n```python\nwith pytest.raises(ParseError) as exc_info:\n    tokenize_yaml(\"\")\nassert exc_info.value.text == \"No content.\"\nassert exc_info.value.code == \"no_content\"\nassert exc_info.value.position == Position(column_no=1, line_no=1, char_index=0)\n```\n\nParseError has attributes: text, code, position? Let me check. In typesystem.base:\n\n```python\nclass Message:\n    def __init__(self, *, text, code, position=None):\n        self.text = text\n        self.code = code\n        self.position = position\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        return self.text == other.text and self.code == other.code and self.position == other.position\n    def __str__(self):\n        ...\n```\n\nParseError(Message) with `messages` property etc.\n\nPosition:\n```python\nclass Position:\n    def __init__(self, line_no, column_no, char_index):\n        ...\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        ...\n```\n\n2. `test_tokenize_yaml_whitespace`: tokenize_yaml(\"   \\n  \") should also raise no_content.\n\n3. `test_tokenize_yaml_bytes`: tokenize_yaml(b\"key: value\") returns DictToken with value {\"key\": \"value\"}.\n\n4. `test_tokenize_yaml_mapping`: tokenize_yaml('{\"a\": 1}') -> DictToken, value {\"a\": 1}. Also check start/end positions and to_string.\n\n5. `test_tokenize_yaml_sequence`: tokenize_yaml(\"[1, 2]\") -> ListToken, value [1, 2].\n\n6. `test_tokenize_yaml_scalar_string`: tokenize_yaml('\"hello\"') -> ScalarToken, value \"hello\".\n\n7. `test_tokenize_yaml_int`: tokenize_yaml(\"123\") -> ScalarToken value 123.\n\n8. `test_tokenize_yaml_float`: tokenize_yaml(\"1.5\") -> ScalarToken value 1.5.\n\n9. `test_tokenize_yaml_bool`: tokenize_yaml(\"true\") -> ScalarToken value True.\n\n10. `test_tokenize_yaml_null`: tokenize_yaml(\"null\") -> ScalarToken value None.\n\n11. `test_tokenize_yaml_parse_error`: tokenize_yaml(\"{invalid\") raises ParseError with code \"parse_error\". E.g. yaml \"a: b\\n  c: d\" or something that causes ScannerError. Let's use something like \"key: [unclosed\" or \"\\t\" (tab causes scanner error). Actually a simple one: `\"{` causes ParserError? Let me think: yaml.load(\"{\") raises ScannerError? Let's test mentally: `yaml.load(\"{\", Loader=SafeLoader)` raises `yaml.scanner.ScannerError: while parsing a flow mapping ... expected '}'`. Hmm, actually \"{\" alone: the scanner scans flow mapping start, then expects a key... The error might be a ParserError: \"while parsing a flow mapping... expected ',' or '}'\". Either way, both are caught.\n\nA reliable ScannerError: a tab character used for indentation: `yaml.load(\"\\ta: 1\", Loader=SafeLoader)` raises ScannerError \"found character '\\\\t' that cannot start any token\". Or `yaml.load(\"a: b\\n\\tc: d\")`. Let me use something simple like `\"{invalid\"`:\n\n`yaml.load(\"{invalid\", SafeLoader)` \u2014 scanning \"{invalid\" \u2014 flow mapping start, then scalar \"invalid\", then end of stream without closing brace \u2192 ParserError \"while parsing a flow mapping ... expected ',' or '}'\". That's a ParserError, which is caught.\n\nTo be safe, I'll use a string that definitely raises ScannerError: `\"a:\\n\\t- b\"`? Hmm. Actually simplest: `\"\\t\"` \u2014 a lone tab. yaml.load(\"\\t\") raises ScannerError: found character '\\t' that cannot start any token. Yes, that's a classic ScannerError.\n\nBut wait \u2014 the empty check: `\"\\t\".strip()` is empty! So it would raise no_content. Need a tab with content: `\"a:\\n\\tb: 1\"`? Hmm, tab indentation: \n\n```\na:\n\tb: 1\n```\n\nThat raises ScannerError \"found character '\\\\t' that cannot start any token\". Let me use `\"a: 1\\n\\tb: 2\"`. Actually the tab appears at start of line 2, which is