id\" is 8 chars, indices 0-7, stream end mark index 8). So position char_index=8, line 1, column 9.\n\nI'll assert code == \"parse_error\" and isinstance(message.position, Position) and maybe char_index == 8. Let me assert char_index == 8 \u2014 reasonably safe. Hmm, actually is the stream-end mark index 8? The StreamEndToken's start_mark is at the end of input, index = len = 8. Yes. But hmm, is the error raised at the stream end token or somewhere else? The parser peeks the token after the scalar \"invalid\" \u2014 that's StreamEndToken at index 8. So problem_mark.index = 8. I'll assert message.position.char_index == 8. Actually, to be safe, maybe just assert it's a Position and code is parse_error. Let me assert char_index == 8 anyway \u2014 it's deterministic. Hmm, but if the parser raises at a different point... Let me think again about \"{invalid\":\n\nScanner tokens: FlowMappingStart '{' (index 0), Scalar 'invalid' (plain, indices 1-7... wait plain scalar ends at index 8? The scalar \"invalid\" spans indices 1-7, end_mark at 8), StreamEnd (index 8).\n\nParser: parse_flow_mapping_key(first=True): checks check_token(FlowMappingEnd)? No. Then... hmm, actually the flow mapping parse:\n\n```python\n    def parse_flow_mapping_key(self, first=False):\n        token = self.get_token()  # consumes '{' on first\n        ...\n```\n\nHmm the details don't matter; the error will be a ParserError with problem_mark at the stream end (index 8) or possibly at the scalar. Let me not over-assert: assert code == \"parse_error\" and message.position is not None. Actually, let me assert `isinstance(message.position, Position)`. That's safe.\n\nHmm, wait. Actually, maybe I should double check whether \"{invalid\" even raises ParserError vs ScannerError. Either way it's caught by the except clause. Fine.\n\nAlternatively, use a cleaner ParserError example: \"a: b\\n- c\" or \"[1, 2\" (unclosed flow sequence). \"[1, 2\" \u2192 ParserError \"expected ',' or ']', but got '<stream end>'\". Either is fine. I'll use \"{invalid\".\n\nHmm, actually, let me reconsider using something even more standard: `tokenize_yaml(\"{\")`. \"{\" alone \u2192 Scanner emits FlowMappingStart, then StreamEnd. Parser: parse_flow_mapping_key \u2192 not FlowMappingEnd, not FlowEntry \u2192 hmm, with empty mapping... Actually \"{\" alone: parser expects a key or '}'. It gets StreamEnd \u2192 ParserError \"expected ',' or '}', but got '<stream end>'\"? Hmm, or maybe \"expected <block end>, ...\"? No \u2014 inside flow mapping. I think \"{\"\" raises ParserError. typesystem's own test uses tokenize_yaml(\"{\") I believe. Let me use \"{invalid\" \u2014 definitely an error.\n\nOK. Now, let me also think about whether I should test `tokenize_yaml` with a document containing multiple documents (\"---\\na: 1\\n---\\nb: 2\")? yaml.load only loads the first document; the second would cause... actually yaml.load with multiple documents raises ComposerError \"expected a single document in the stream\". ComposerError is NOT caught by the except clause (only ScannerError, ParserError) \u2192 it would propagate as ComposerError. Not needed for coverage; skip.\n\nNow \u2014 the \"branches\" aspect. Branches in the code:\n1. `if isinstance(content, bytes)` \u2014 both branches: bytes test + str tests. \u2713\n2. `if not str_content.strip()` \u2014 both: empty tests + non-empty tests. \u2713\n3. try/except \u2014 both: success tests + error tests. \u2713\n4. assert yaml is not None \u2014 both: normal tests (pass) + monkeypatch test (fail). \u2713\n\nLines: all constructor functions covered by respective tests. \u2713\n\nNow, about state pollution: the CustomSafeLoader is defined inside the function and constructors are added to it per-call \u2014 no global state modified. The monkeypatch test restores yaml. No file/system state. Good.\n\nOne more thing: importing `from typesystem.tokenize.tokenize_yaml import tokenize_yaml` \u2014 but also the module object for monkeypatching. \n\nLet me a