me recall typesystem/tokenize/tokens.py more carefully. From the actual repo (encode/typesystem, master):\n\n```python\nimport typing\n\nfrom typesystem.base import Position\n\n\nclass Token:\n    def __init__(self, value, start_index, end_index, content=None):\n        assert content is not None or isinstance(value, (str, bytes)), (\n            \"Must pass either content=..., or value must be a string.\"\n        )\n        self._value = value\n        self._start_index = start_index\n        self._end_index = end_index\n        self._content = content\n\n    def __repr__(self) -> str:\n        return \"%s(%r)\" % (self.__class__.__name__, self.value)\n\n    def __eq__(self, other: typing.Any) -> bool:\n        return self.value == other.value\n\n    def __hash__(self) -> int:\n        return hash(self.value)\n\n    @property\n    def value(self) -> typing.Any:\n        return self.get_value()\n\n    @property\n    def start(self) -> Position:\n        return self._get_position(self._start_index)\n\n    @property\n    def end(self) -> Position:\n        return self._get_position(self._end_index)\n\n    def _get_position(self, index: int) -> Position:\n        return Position(\n            line_no=self._content.count(\"\\n\", 0, index) + 1,\n            column_no=index - self._content.rfind(\"\\n\", 0, index),\n            char_index=index,\n        )\n\n    def get_value(self) -> typing.Any:\n        return self._value\n\n    def is_scalar(self) -> bool:\n        return isinstance(self, ScalarToken)\n\n    def is_dict(self) -> bool:\n        return isinstance(self, DictToken)\n\n    def is_list(self) -> bool:\n        return isinstance(self, ListToken)\n\n    def to_string(self) -> str:\n        return self._content[self._start_index : self._end_index + 1]\n\n\nclass ScalarToken(Token):\n    def has_value(self, value: str) -> bool:\n        return str(self.value) == value\n\n\nclass DictToken(Token):\n    def get_value(self) -> typing.Any:\n        return {\n            key.get_value(): value.get_value() for key, value in self._value.items()\n        }\n\n\nclass ListToken(Token):\n    def get_value(self) -> typing.Any:\n        return [value.get_value() for value in self._value]\n```\n\nI'm fairly confident Token defines both __eq__ and __hash__ (hash of value). OK good.\n\nBut wait \u2014 there's a subtlety with construct_mapping and duplicate keys: if two keys have equal values (e.g. {\"a\": 1, \"a\": 2}), the ScalarTokens hash equal \u2192 dict dedupes. Fine.\n\nAnother subtlety: SafeConstructor.construct_mapping calls self.flatten_mapping(node) for merge keys (<<). flatten_mapping calls construct_object on keys... For simple YAML, no merges. Fine.\n\nNow \u2014 important: does `loader.construct_mapping(node)` in our custom construct_mapping recurse properly? Yes: BaseConstructor.construct_mapping \u2192 construct_object(key_node) \u2192 looks up constructor by tag \u2192 our custom constructors \u2192 returns Tokens. \n\nBut hmm, one thing: `construct_object` has a `deep` parameter and caching (self.constructed_objects). For non-deep construction of nested collections, PyYAML constructs generators... Actually for mappings/sequences, construct_object returns the generator-based partial? Let me think: BaseConstructor.construct_object:\n\n```python\ndef construct_object(self, node, deep=False):\n    if node in self.constructed_objects:\n        return self.constructed_objects[node]\n    if deep:\n        old_deep = self.deep_construct\n        self.deep_construct = True\n    if node in self.recursive_objects:\n        raise ConstructorError(...)\n    self.recursive_objects[node] = None\n    constructor = None\n    tag_suffix = None\n    if node.tag in self.yaml_constructors:\n        constructor = self.yaml_constructors[node.tag]\n    else:\n        ...\n    if tag_suffix is None:\n        data = constructor(self, node)\n    else:\n        data = constructor(self, tag_suffix, node)\n    if isinstance(data, types.GeneratorType):\n        generator = data\n        data = next(generator)\n        if self.deep_construct:\n            for dummy in generator:\n                pass\n        else:\n            self.state_generators.append(generator)\n    self.constructed_objects[node] = data\n    del self.recursive_objects[node]\n    if deep:\n        self.deep_construct = old_deep\n    return data\n```\n\nOur constructors return Token objects directly (not generators), so data = Token. Good. And nested nodes get constructed via the same registry since we call loader.construct_mapping/construct_sequence which call construct_object on children. \n\nWait, but there's a catch: BaseConstructor.construct_mapping is called with deep=False by default from our construct_mapping (`loader.construct_mapping(node)`). Then for value nodes that are collections, construct_object(value_node, deep=False) \u2192 our construct_mapping returns DictToken immediately (not a generator), so no deferred construction. All good \u2014 nested tokens are fully constructed.\n\nHmm, wait: actually SafeConstructor.construct_mapping signature: `def construct_mapping(self, node, deep=False)`. Our call `loader.construct_mapping(node)` passes deep=False. Fine.\n\nNow, `flatten_mapping` \u2014 SafeConstructor.construct_mapping:\n\n```python\ndef construct_mapping(self, node, deep=False):\n    if isinstance(node, MappingNode):\n        self.flatten_mapping(node)\n    return BaseConstructor.construct_mapping(self, node, deep=deep)\n```\n\nflatten_mapping handles merge keys '<<'. For keys with tag merge... it calls construct_object(key_node) for merge keys. Not relevant for simple tests.\n\nOK now \u2014 one more potential gotcha: `construct_scalar` is registered for DEFAULT_SCALAR_TAG which is \"tag:yaml.org,2002:str\". But PyYAML's resolver resolves plain scalars to specific tags (int, float, bool, null, timestamp, etc.) via implicit resolution. Quoted scalars get the str tag. Plain \"hello\" \u2192 str tag. Plain \"123\" \u2192